I've written the session plan: two task files plus the issue responses. The commit didn't happen because .gitignore excludes session_plan/. The files are on disk for the next phase, which presumably reads them there.

Task 1: Fixes #1198 (product, Bedrock maxTokens). yoagent 0.27.9 now sends a valid cap above the thinking budget, but only when thinking is on. yoyo's own workaround (from #1173) sends 64000 every time, thinking on or off. So deleting yoyo's copy would probably drop the cap entirely for runs without thinking. That's a change users would see, not a cleanup.
• The task records what yoyo sends at the existing recording stub before deciding: thinking on/off × with/without a user max_tokens, with the workaround and without it, 8 rows.
• It then keeps one rule and says in the commit which one wins and why, in user terms.
• It keeps the stub test as the regression test @yuanhao asked for.
• It re-checks that the guessed-limits note still matches what is sent.
• One leftover from my own comment on #1198 is probably still true: /side and architect agents send no maxTokens on Bedrock. If so, it gets its own issue before the push.
• No request goes to AWS.

Task 2: Part of #1202, step 1 (product, safety). Every bash command gets parsed with tree-sitter-bash in a new src/safety/parse_tree.rs, and the existing safety checks run on each command pulled out of $(), backticks, subshells, strings and heredocs.
• The old raw-text check stays as a second net, so nothing refused today becomes allowed.
• A parse error, a command name built from an expansion, or a parse that hits the node budget goes to confirmation, never to allow.
• In piped -p mode that confirmation doesn't actually block: the command is logged and run anyway. The commit has to say so plainly.
• safety.rs is at its size cap with no room left, so the wiring is a few lines and the cap entry gets updated with a reason.
• The commit has to state: why this crate over the alternatives, binary size and startup time before and after, and how many of my own real past commands newly go to confirmation.
• #1193 may close here if its census of missed shapes empties (18 today). #1196 stays open, because the raw-text net that causes its false positive is still in place.

